!-----------------------------------------------------------------------
!
!> \brief Correlation splitting, weak part
!>
!> The regular model for alpha-beta electron correlation is based on
!> the assumption that the system is weakly correlated. This model
!> breaks down when an orbital occupation number passes 1/2, as a sign
!> change happens at that point. This leads to discontinuities in the
!> potential energy surface.
!>
!> Obviously this problem should not come as a surprise as at an
!> occupation of 1/2 the orbital is maximally strongly correlated and
!> weakly correlated at all. A strongly correlated model can easily be
!> derived for H2 in a minimal basis set. But this model is clearly
!> in general when the occupation numbers are close to 0 and 1.
!>
!> Therefore we need something that smoothly combines the strong and
!> weak correlation models. It should ensure that the weak correlation
!> model vanishes at occupation numbers of 1/2. The strong correlation
!> model should vanish when the occupation numbers are 0 or 1. Here
!> we implement such partition functions. Obviously the sum of both
!> parts should alway be 1.
!>
!> In principle various such partitionings are possible. If \f$p\f$ is
!> the orbital occupation number then one possibility would be
!> \f{eqnarray}{
!>   1 &=& \cos^2(\pi p) + \sin^2(\pi p)
!> \f}
!> where the cosine part is the weak correlation prefactor. This
!> partitioning would preserve the weak correlation model almost exactly
!> close to occupations of 0 and 1.
!>
!> Unfortunately we have at least one case (LiH)2 where the weak
!> correlation model underestimates the correlation effects. So maybe
!> we need a larger fraction of strong correlation close to occupations
!> of 0 and 1. The following partitioning would go in that direction
!> \f{eqnarray}{
!>    s &=& 4 p (1-p) \\
!>    w &=& 1 - s \\
!>    1 &=& w + s
!> \f}
!>
      function noft_part_strong(p) result(s)
      implicit none
      !> The orbital occupation number
      real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: p
      !> The fraction corresponding to the strong correlation model
      real(kind=dp) :: s
!
      s = 4.0_dp * p * (1.0_dp - p)
!
      end function noft_part_strong
!
!-----------------------------------------------------------------------
!
!> \brief the weak correlation part
!>
!> This definition is universal as the weak plus the strong part
!> must be 1.
!>
      function noft_part_weak(p) result(w)
      implicit none
      !> The orbital occupation number
      real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: p
      !> The fraction corresponding to the weak correlation model
      real(kind=dp) :: w
!
      w = 1.0_dp - noft_part_strong(p)
!
      end function noft_part_weak
!
!-----------------------------------------------------------------------
